---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

const toc = [
	{ id: 'project-scope', label: '1. What a Municipal Water & Fire Scope Actually Includes' },
	{ id: 'standards-regimes', label: '2. Standards Regimes in the Region: What the Tender May Require' },
	{ id: 'one-purchase-order', label: '3. How a Single Purchase Order Reduces Coordination Risk' },
	{ id: 'selection-matrix', label: '4. Selection Matrix: Component, Typical Spec, Recommended Model' },
	{ id: 'specification-documentation', label: '5. Specification & Documentation to Request' },
	{ id: 'procurement-checklist', label: '6. Municipal Procurement Checklist' },
	{ id: 'faq', label: 'Frequently Asked Questions' },
	{ id: 'sources', label: 'Sources' }
];

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'Can one supplier actually cover hydrants, valves, hose and backflow for a municipal project?',
		answer: 'Yes. Wanlian Fluid runs its own production lines in Quanzhou, with selected items produced by qualified manufacturing partners, and consolidates hydrants, gate and control valves, fire hose, couplings and backflow preventers under a single purchase order. That means one documentation trail, one inspection point and one accountable manufacturer for the whole kit.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Which standards will my LatAm tender actually name?',
		answer: 'It depends on the country and on whether the scope follows a US, European or national reference. Mexico tenders frequently cite NOM; Brazil cites ABNT; US-spec or re-export work may cite AWWA, NFPA or UL/FM; many projects reference EN or ISO. Always read the standard named in the specification first — compliance with a specific standard must be confirmed per model with test reports and certificates before ordering.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Does Wanlian Fluid hold AWWA, NOM or ABNT certification?',
		answer: 'Wanlian Fluid operates under an ISO 9001 quality-management system and pressure-tests every unit before shipment. Standard-specific qualification is handled per model: send the standard on your specification and the team will confirm conformance, with matching test reports and certificates, for the exact models you order before you commit.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What does "per-unit testing" mean in practice for my order?',
		answer: 'It means the shell of every individual unit is pressure-tested and then air-leakage tested before it is packaged — not a sample taken from the batch. For safety-critical fire equipment this is the responsible baseline, and it is documented per unit so your inspector can trace it.'
	},
	{
		question: 'How long until I have a quote for a full kit?',
		answer: 'Send your specification and destination. Wanlian Fluid commits to replying with an itemized quote within 12 hours, breaking out each component, size, pressure class and standard reference so you can compare against your tender line by line.'
	}
];

const productRows = [
	{ slug: 'overground-fire-hydrant-ss100', name: 'Overground Antifreeze Fire Hydrant (SS100/65)', img: '/assets/products/overground-fire-hydrant-ss100.webp', desc: 'Outdoor hydrant for municipal fire and water networks' },
	{ slug: 'resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x', name: 'Ductile Iron Resilient Seat Flanged Gate Valve (Z41X)', img: '/assets/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x.webp', desc: 'Full-bore isolation valve for water mains and fire pipelines' },
	{ slug: 'fire-hose-pvc-rubber-lining-8-65', name: 'PVC Rubber Lining Layflat Fire Hose (Model 8-65)', img: '/assets/products/fire-hose-pvc-rubber-lining-8-65.jpg', desc: 'Rubber-lined layflat hose for firefighting and water transfer' }
];
---

<Layout
	title="Municipal Water & Fire Infrastructure in Latin America: A B2B Procurement Guide (2026)"
	description="Municipal fire hydrants, gate valves, fire hose and backflow protection for Latin American water supply network projects — a 2026 B2B procurement guide."
	type="article"
	faq={faq}
	breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/blog/' }, { name: 'Municipal Water & Fire, LatAm' }]}
>
	<main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14 mb-16">
		<nav class="text-xs text-gray-400 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-6 flex gap-2">
			<a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a><span>/</span>
			<a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a><span>/</span>
			<span class="text-gray-600">Municipal Water &amp; Fire, LatAm</span>
		</nav>

		<article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-gray-100 p-6 sm:p-10">
			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Municipal Infrastructure &amp; B2B Procurement</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">Municipal Water &amp; Fire Infrastructure in Latin America: A B2B Procurement Guide (2026)</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">A municipal water &amp; fire project in Latin America is rarely a single purchase. One tender asks for overground hydrants at street corners, isolation gate valves in the main, pressure-reducing valves at zone boundaries, backflow preventers protecting the drinking-water supply, and fire hose with couplings for the local brigade — each often quoted by a different supplier. That fragmentation is where projects slip: mismatched interfaces, split liability, and a documentation trail no single party owns. This guide shows you how to build that scope as one coordinated set, how to read the standards regimes a LatAm tender may actually name, and how to specify and document it so a single manufacturer can quote, test and ship the whole kit under one purchase order.</p>

			<div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
				{productRows.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-32 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<h2 id="project-scope" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. What a Municipal Water &amp; Fire Scope Actually Includes</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Across Mexico, Colombia, Peru, Chile, Brazil and Central America, a municipal water &amp; fire package normally falls into five component families. Treating them as one coordinated set — rather than five separate line items sourced on different timelines — is the single most effective way to reduce the risk of interface failures on site:</p>
			<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Hydrants.</strong> Overground hydrants at street and block locations, plus anti-collision or pressure-regulating variants on high-traffic corridors. These are the most visible deliverable in the scope.</li>
				<li><strong>Isolation valves.</strong> Gate valves (resilient-seat, full-bore) in the main and at hydrant branches, and butterfly valves where space or weight is the constraint.</li>
				<li><strong>Pressure &amp; zone control.</strong> Adjustable pressure-reducing valves at the boundary of each pressure zone so the network stays within its design range.</li>
				<li><strong>Backflow protection.</strong> Anti-pollution backflow preventers on connections that could siphon or backpressure the drinking-water supply.</li>
				<li><strong>Fire hose &amp; couplings.</strong> Layflat attack hose with matching couplings for the fire brigade and for hydrant-to-pumper operations.</li>
			</ol>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The point is not to buy everything from one brand — it is to make sure the five families are <em>designed and documented to work together</em>. A hydrant, a gate valve, a pressure-reducing valve, a backflow preventer and a length of hose all connect to the same mains and the same operating procedures, so their flanges, threads, pressure classes and materials must be compatible before anything is cast.</p>

			<h2 id="standards-regimes" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. Standards Regimes in the Region: What the Tender May Require</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Latin America is a patchwork of standards regimes. The tender itself decides which one governs your scope — read the specification first, then map each component to it. The table below is a map of the regimes you are likely to encounter, framed as <em>what the tender may require</em>, not as a list of certifications any supplier holds:</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Regime</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Where it appears</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">What it typically covers</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">What you confirm per order</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">AWWA C502 / C515</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">US-spec projects, re-export, some private utilities</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Dry-barrel fire hydrants; resilient-seated gate valves</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Per-model conformance, with test reports and certificates before ordering</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">NOM / NR (Mexico)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Mexican municipal and federal tenders</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">National water and fire-protection product requirements</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Which NOM clause the tender names; per-model verification against it</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">ABNT (Brazil)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Brazilian municipal and industrial projects</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Brazilian standards for water supply and fire equipment</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">ABNT reference per component; per-model confirmation with reports</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">EN / ISO (EN 14384, ISO 5208, ISO 7165)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Colombia, Peru, Chile and consultant-driven specs</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Hydrant design; valve testing and pressure-rating methods</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Pressure class and test method match the specification</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">NFPA 1961 / 13 / 24</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Fire-brigade and hydrant-installation references</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Fire hose performance; sprinkler systems; hydrant installation</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Hose coupling type and hydrant installation details named in scope</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">UL / FM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Where US-spec insurance or re-export is involved</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Listed/approved fire-protection products</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Only if the contract requires it; verified per model at the issuing body</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The practical rule is the same in every market: <strong>compliance with a specific standard must be confirmed per model with test reports and certificates before ordering.</strong> A generic brochure does not prove a given hydrant or valve conforms to the clause your tender cites — a per-model test record does. Our <a href="/guides/fire-hydrant-valve-standards-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire hydrant &amp; valve standards guide</a> explains how to read a standard against a test report line by line.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Couplings add a second layer. Specify the interface your brigade actually uses — Storz (widespread in the region), NH threads, or instantaneous/BS 336 — and have the supplier confirm it in writing. Our <a href="/products/storz-coupling-2-5inch-aluminum/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">2.5&quot; aluminum Storz coupling</a> is a common match for hydrant-to-pumper operations; the <a href="/guides/how-to-choose-a-fire-hydrant/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire hydrant selection guide</a> walks through hydrant types, pressure classes and connection standards.</p>

			<h2 id="one-purchase-order" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. How a Single Purchase Order Reduces Coordination Risk</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">When hydrants, valves, hose and backflow protection come from one manufacturer under one PO, you remove the failure modes that most often delay municipal projects:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>One accountable party.</strong> If a hydrant branch and its isolation valve do not match, the dispute is with one supplier — not three.</li>
				<li><strong>Coordinated interfaces.</strong> Flange faces, threads, pressure classes and materials are cross-checked across the whole kit before production, so components arrive ready to connect.</li>
				<li><strong>One documentation trail.</strong> Per-unit test records, certificates and export documents sit in a single file that your inspector and the contracting authority can review together.</li>
				<li><strong>One quality gate.</strong> Every unit in the kit is shell pressure-tested and air-leakage tested before shipment, under an ISO 9001 quality-management system.</li>
				<li><strong>One inspection point.</strong> A third-party inspection (e.g. SGS or Bureau Veritas) can be staged against one shipment instead of several.</li>
				<li><strong>OEM &amp; branding options.</strong> Where the tender requires the contracting authority's or your brand, custom branding and export packaging are coordinated on the same PO.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">None of this depends on any single certification claim. It depends on the manufacturer actually building and testing the whole set. That is what Wanlian Fluid's own production lines — with selected items made by qualified partners and valve bodies CNC-machined in-house — are set up to do, with a published range of 6 product lines and 43+ in-stock models. To understand the vendor side of this, read our <a href="/guides/how-to-verify-chinese-valve-supplier/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">guide to verifying a Chinese valve &amp; fire-equipment supplier</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="selection-matrix" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Selection Matrix: Component, Typical Spec, Recommended Model</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Use this matrix as a starting point for your RFQ. Each row pairs a scope component with the kind of specification a LatAm tender commonly names and a Wanlian Fluid model that addresses it. Confirm size, pressure class and standard reference against <em>your</em> specification — the per-model qualification note applies to every row:</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Component</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Typical tender spec</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Recommended Wanlian model</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Note</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-200">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Overground hydrant</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Ductile iron, outdoor, street/block locations</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/overground-fire-hydrant-ss100/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Overground Antifreeze Fire Hydrant (SS100/65)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">AWWA C502 / EN 14384 framing per model</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Anti-collision / pressure-regulating hydrant</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">High-traffic corridors, vehicle protection</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/anti-collision-pressure-regulating-outdoor-hydrant-ssft100/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Anti-Collision Pressure-Regulating Outdoor Fire Hydrant (SSFT100/65)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Confirm impact and pressure-regulating spec per model</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Monitored / smart hydrant</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Network condition monitoring</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/iot-smart-ground-fire-hydrant-ss100zn/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">IoT Smart Ground Fire Hydrant (SS100/65-1.6ZN)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Confirm communication and telemetry scope</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Isolation gate valve</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Ductile iron, resilient seat, full-bore, PN16 or ANSI</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/resilient-seat-flanged-gate-valve-z41x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Ductile Iron Resilient Seat Flanged Gate Valve (Z41X)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">ISO 5208 test method; class matched to system pressure</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Pressure-reducing valve</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Hydraulic, adjustable, at zone boundary</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/adjustable-pressure-reducing-valve-500x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Hydraulic Adjustable Pressure Reducing Valve (500X)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Set inlet/outlet pressure range from the network design</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Backflow preventer</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Anti-pollution, drinking-water protection</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/anti-pollution-backflow-preventer-hs41x/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">Anti-Pollution Backflow Preventer (HS41X-16)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Confirm backflow category the tender requires</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Fire hose</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Polyester jacket, vulcanized rubber lining, layflat</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/fire-hose-pvc-rubber-lining-8-65/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">PVC Rubber Lining Layflat Fire Hose (Model 8-65)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">NFPA 1961 framing; coupling type specified</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Couplings</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Storz (German standard), 2.5&quot;</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700"><a href="/products/storz-coupling-2-5inch-aluminum/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">2.5&quot; Aluminum Storz Coupling (German Standard)</a></td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Must match hose ends and brigade equipment</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Every model in this matrix is subject to the same rule: <strong>compliance with a specific standard must be confirmed per model with test reports and certificates before ordering.</strong> Send the exact clause and size from your tender and the engineering team will return a line-by-line confirmation rather than assume it.</p>

			<h2 id="specification-documentation" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Specification &amp; Documentation to Request</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">For a municipal kit, put these items in the purchase order so they are contractually due — not optional afterthoughts:</p>
			<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li><strong>Per-unit test records.</strong> Shell pressure test and air-leakage test for each unit, traceable to the serial/lot, so your inspector can match a record to a physical item.</li>
				<li><strong>ISO 9001 certificate.</strong> Verify it with the issuing body and confirm the scope covers the product categories in your order.</li>
				<li><strong>Per-model standard confirmation.</strong> Where your tender cites AWWA, NOM, ABNT, EN or NFPA, a written conformance statement against the named clause for each model.</li>
				<li><strong>Materials documentation.</strong> Body material (for example ductile iron versus cast iron) and lining details — these drive price and lifetime and should be stated, not implied.</li>
				<li><strong>OEM &amp; branding confirmation.</strong> Custom branding, markings and export packaging exactly as the tender or your brand requires.</li>
				<li><strong>Export documents.</strong> Commercial invoice, packing list and the shipping documents your destination requires, coordinated on the same PO.</li>
			</ol>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The same documentation discipline protects you when you verify the supplier itself. Our <a href="/guides/b2b-valve-sourcing-checklist/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">B2B valve sourcing checklist</a> lists the vendor-level checks — legal entity, ISO scope, per-unit testing, third-party inspection — that sit behind this list.</p>

			<h2 id="procurement-checklist" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Municipal Procurement Checklist</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Before you issue the PO, work through this list. It is designed to catch the coordination failures that cost municipal projects the most time:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
				<li>Each of the five component families is named with size, pressure class and quantity.</li>
				<li>The standard named in the tender is identified for each component, not assumed.</li>
				<li>Interfaces (flange, thread, coupling) are cross-checked across hydrants, valves and hose.</li>
				<li>One manufacturer is specified for the coordinated set, or compatibility is confirmed in writing across suppliers.</li>
				<li>Per-unit test records and ISO 9001 certificate are written into the contract as deliverables.</li>
				<li>Third-party inspection is staged against one shipment, before dispatch.</li>
				<li>OEM/branding and export packaging are confirmed where the tender requires them.</li>
				<li>The quote-response and lead-time commitments are recorded in the purchase order.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A tender that specifies the whole set, names its standards, and requires the documentation is a tender a single manufacturer can win on coordination as well as price. That is the position Wanlian Fluid is built for: quote the set, test every unit, and ship it under one PO.</p>

			<!-- FAQ Section -->
			<h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
			<div class="space-y-4">
				{faq.map(item => (
					<details class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 p-5 open:border-brand-red/30">
						<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-gray-800 cursor-pointer list-none">
							<span>{item.question}</span>
							<span class="text-brand-blue text-xl transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">{item.answer}</p>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>

			<!-- Sources -->
			<h2 id="sources" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">Sources</h2>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed space-y-1.5">
